ed egalitarian community  um and going through something together  and so we ended up repositioning the  scene so that we're sort of building  towards that and it becomes a kind of  critical  step on the road to the journey that the  audience goes on along with jim  i think another reason that the film  that this  uh scene is sort of in the middle of  this section of the film is  you really have to the audience needs to  know these people in some way as  individuals so this is a group of people  that you're introduced  to gradually over the course of the  first i don't know  five six minutes and so that the  audiences really  really knows them and cares about them  and i think if you start with this scene  it would be just so  sort of shocking and disorienting that  it would  actually not draw the audience into the  film  that's incredible  you know i would love to have somebody  to wash your balls  right i can do it myself for me so you  know i don't really care  but there are other people who can you  know and they have to have it done for  him  there's a moment in the scene here would  leave me  where the tenor of the scene shifts and  it's very intentional the music shifts  the mood shifts  and what we're kind of doing is we're  we're bursting the bubble  we thought of this as a really important  moment in the narrative where we had  created the bubble of camp and at this  at this moment we're kind of puncturing  the bubble a bit and we're  starting to see how the campers are um  dealing with the with the outside world  i think actually what happened  was that people were having fun we were  working together  um as a whole unit washing and cleaning  and showering and doing things that  we've never done before and  it's really very different kind of a  thing and i have to go shower some  people  i'll see you later it's just so sort of  loving and if there's a line in it where  judy says  judy heumann says um it's the first time  i had to get used to somebody other than  my mother  taking care of me and you just feel that  in the way  that the campers and the counselors are  reacting to one another  in that swimming pool it's just  something very very beautiful  and that was something that i think we  saw right away in that  footage there were people there that  would  help me get dressed and undress and go  to the bathroom and shower get in and  out of the pool  and again you know i talk about humor or  i think about humor the attitude  of paul who had multiple disabilities  going on  modeled for me this wonderful way of  just being  he was just like oh well and it helped  me feel better about myself  there's a loose kind of disorganized  flowing sense of community  and that footage that the people's video  theater did such a beautiful job of  capturing  and it couldn't be over cut we had to  kind of feel that and i think the crabs  epidemic  gave us an opportunity to do that but at  the same time it's so intentionally cut  together and one of the beautiful things  that mary you did  was just build in these characters that  we kind of see  throughout the film over and over again  we don't just see them but we hear their  names  so even though they're minor characters  they're just people at the camp and they  never become a story point or part of  the narrative  but they become a part of what starts to  feel like a really multi-dimensional  world that we're immersed in  and so it boggles my mind actually the  extent to which you could take  this old footage and really make it feel  like a present day  immersive experience back home  i have to be careful who i said things  to  because that was a way of surviving  there were survival skills that you had  i had to be very very very careful not  to be disrespectful  not to look a white man in his eye you  had to do those things you had to be  mindful of that so at the end of  lionel's discussion of that comparing  the discrimination  that he experienced as a black man in  the south to the discrimination that he  saw people with disabilities facing when  they went into town to get an ice cream  say  i got so much things i don't know if i'm  really blue  cause i got the one time one time  whatever it is maybe we should start off  with um  overprotectiveness which i really hate  does anybody want to start my parents  are great  but sometimes i hate them because  they're too great and they're too  protective of me  which is really powerful because it's a  scene that literally was directed by the  campers in 1971 right jim  yeah when the people's video theater  came and taught us the first time they  said  look here's who we are help us make a  film about your camp  and so one of the things we asked was  we'd like to make a vid a film  about video that was near a lexicon  a film about uh our parents and how  they're  over protective and such and so  this scene which we call message to  parents  is a result of them asking us giving us  agency like  we didn't really experience from people  before well  some of the things like just everybody  else depends her parents for like  warning stuff but like  like she's the person that orders  special supplies when i'll need it and  stuff  and find a position where i'm unable to  do something  you know like she's gonna have to do it  and so like if you keep on [ __ ] you  this was a scene that the moment we saw  it jim and i  knew that we would be building a whole  first part of the film that was set at  the camp  up to this moment because it feels like  a moment of transformation and it feels  like a moment where  the community at camp has gelled and  people have moved into a  kind of a new way of thinking and being  this scene didn't really work until we  had  all the other scenes building up to it  working such that it felt like that  evolution towards a community had  happened but one of the things that i  really love about  this scene is how mary you pay such  incredible attention to it  yeah i think in the beginning you didn't  have a sense of the physical space  or it was sort of confusing because the  focus was primarily  sort of on jim and then obviously on  nancy  and then steve at the end but it was  hard to sort of  see the whole group so we just put a  couple of shots in  so you really understood that it was a  circle of people  sitting around a table having this  conversation it feels egalitarian right  i mean it feels like the spirit of the  kind of conversation that they're having  where they would  listen to nancy and that was something  that became really important later on in  the film when we go to the 504 sit-in  and we see that same kind of ethos  and set of values really expressed in  the sit-in and what made it successful  and what made the laws change and that  all had to be  communicated in this scene  yeah when we first watched this scene  we're like everybody else there we're  not quite sure  we really don't know what nancy is  saying and then we realized that  it would be interesting for our audience  to have the same experience  to be uncomfortable maybe not knowing  what she's saying because you know we  have  done subtitles for a lot of other people  who had disability affected speech  and that some folks would find hard to  understand  and it was the right decision i think  yeah when we showed this footage early  on to people they said there's no way  you can put this in the film it's going  to make people too uncomfortable  and so one of the beautiful things i  think about the way it's edited is that  you see these kind of like loving really  attentive  looks from multiple campers as nancy's  talking  and by the time you get to the scene  because  of the way that sense of community has  been constructed you  you have the patience as a viewer to  know already that you should be  respectful and listen and that nancy is  saying something  important and worth waiting for i think  um because of the way this scene is  constructed  the the